  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential mechanism by which isorhamnetin 3-O-2''-glucosylrhamnoside interacts with VMRC-LCP, through a series of target interactions involving protein-tyrosine phosphatase LC-PTP and other related proteins. The final scores from the three paths indicate a consistent and plausible pathway, with the drug targeting Protein-tyrosine phosphatase LC-PTP, which in turn targets VMRC-LCP. This suggests that the drug may modulate VMRC-LCP through a phosphatase-dependent mechanism, potentially influencing cellular signaling pathways. The shared involvement of Protein-tyrosine phosphatase LC-PTP across the three paths strengthens the evidence for a common target, while the distinct targets of the drug (Hepatocyte and Ginsenoside R10) may indicate a broader biological role. The integration of these findings supports a mechanistic link between the compound and VMRC-LCP, though further experimental validation is needed to confirm the exact nature of the interaction.",
